WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.333
Scalability principles.

2
00:00:02.633 --> 00:00:06.420
Going from one user to ten thousand is not about a faster server.

3
00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.633
It is about knowing what breaks first.

4
00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:11.667
There are two ways to handle more load.

5
00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:14.506
Vertical scaling means a bigger machine.

6
00:00:14.506 --> 00:00:17.933
Horizontal means more machines behind a load balancer.

7
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:23.000
Vertical needs no code changes, but it is simple only until you hit the ceiling.

8
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:28.567
Horizontal can grow almost forever, but it demands one discipline, which we'll see next.

9
00:00:28.867 --> 00:00:32.433
So vertical is the quick fix, and horizontal is what lasts.

10
00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:35.933
Here is the discipline: statelessness.

11
00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:37.833
Requests come in from users.

12
00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:40.833
The load balancer sends each one to any replica.

13
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:45.767
The replicas keep nothing in their own memory, so any of them can serve any request.

14
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:49.487
State moves down a layer, into Redis or a database.

15
00:00:49.487 --> 00:00:51.633
The app tier becomes disposable.

16
00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:54.433
Here is the difference in code.

17
00:00:54.733 --> 00:01:00.633
A sessions dictionary in app memory dies with the pod, and breaks as soon as you run two replicas.

18
00:01:00.933 --> 00:01:05.803
Instead, we read and write the conversation history in Redis, keyed by session.

19
00:01:05.803 --> 00:01:08.700
Any replica can pick up where another left off.

20
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.433
The other half of the skill is bottleneck thinking.

21
00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:13.700
First, measure.

22
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.467
Then find the narrowest component, because the system scales exactly that far.

23
00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:20.331
Fix it, and measure again.

24
00:01:20.331 --> 00:01:23.100
The bottleneck moves, but it never disappears.

25
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:27.600
Picture scaling a chat API to ten thousand users.

26
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:33.323
The first wall is the provider's tokens per minute limit, long before your CPU sweats.

27
00:01:33.323 --> 00:01:34.900
Queuing and caching help.

28
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.594
Next the vector database struggles with query volume.

29
00:01:38.594 --> 00:01:40.067
Read replicas fix that.

30
00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:44.336
Finally your workers run out of open connections while streaming.

31
00:01:44.336 --> 00:01:46.046
Add more stateless replicas.

32
00:01:46.046 --> 00:01:47.633
Find the bottleneck first.

33
00:01:48.833 --> 00:01:49.670
Let's recap.

34
00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:53.367
Vertical scaling is simple, horizontal scaling lasts.

35
00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:55.600
Horizontal needs stateless apps.

36
00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.467
So state lives in Redis or a database.

37
00:01:58.767 --> 00:02:01.407
And always measure to find the bottleneck.

38
00:02:01.407 --> 00:02:03.167
Next up: caching strategies.
